   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In May 2020, the Confederation of Trade Unions of Armenia (CTUA) submitted proposals on tele-work, pay during temporary idling periods and working time during the crises caused by the COVID-19 pandemic to the Republican Tripartite Commission (RTC). Most of the proposals were reflected in the Labour Code amendments adopted by the Parliament in May 2020. Amendments provide more flexibility in reporting of late arrival at workplaces (including prohibition of disciplinary action) given restricted public transport as part of the lockdown measures. In July 2020, CTUA developed and submitted a policy position to RTC on shortcomings in the State&apos;s response measures to the crisis. Recommendations have informed the Government&apos;s steps to address the crises. In October 2021, CTUA submitted a policy position on extending social protection measures to vulnerable categories of workers to RTC and the national SDGs Council (CTUA is a member). The position was informed by CTUA&apos;s analysis of the COVID-19 pandemic&apos;s impact on working conditions in the informal economy and in precarious employment. In December 2021, CTUA&apos;s policy proposals were discussed and recommendations were submitted to the Government.

In July-August 2020, the Branch Union of Miners and Metalworkers negotiated amendments to OSH components of the collective bargaining agreements with the managements of JSC &quot;Rusal-Armenia&quot;, JSC &quot;Clean Iron&quot;, JSC &quot;Copper-Molybdenum plant&quot; addressing OSH threats caused by the pandemic. Amendments also guaranteed furloughed workers 2/3 of their wages.

In February-April 2021, the Agriculture and Healthcare sector trade unions developed policy positions regarding the government&apos;s COVID-19 crises mitigation measures for their respective sectors. A set of recommendations to better address workers&apos; concerns have been discussed and submitted to the line ministries. In July-August 2021, they were discussed by the consultative bodies of both ministries which regularly meet with social par The ILO provided technical support to CTUA to develop policy positions towards the measures taken by the Government in response to the socio-economic crisis caused by COVID-19. 

During April-July 2020, four regional webinars were delivered on trade unions&apos; response strategies towards the COVID-19 pandemic:
&#x2022;	Disrupting Lives, Economies and Societies: Challenges and Prospects for Trade Union Actions.
&#x2022;	Stimulating economy and employment during COVID-19 pandemic.
&#x2022;	Supporting enterprises, jobs and incomes in times of crises.
&#x2022;	Relying on Social Dialogue for Solutions in times of crises.

These webinars informed CTUA&#x2018;s policy position and proposals submitted to the RTC and subsequently influenced the amendments to the Labour Code adopted in May 2020. The same activities have informed the metalworkers and miners&apos; trade unions negotiations with their respective employers.

The ILO supported the analysis of the impact of COVID-19 on workers in the agriculture and healthcare sectors in February-April 2021. 

Similarly, in March-May 2021, the ILO supported the analysis of the impact of COVID-19 on workers in mining and metallurgy sector. 

The ILO provided policy advice to develop proposals for initiating extension of social protection to workers in the informal economy and precarious employment

In July 2020, seven members of the Executive Council of the CTUA received training on SDGs, Voluntary National Review (VNR) processes, engagement in the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UNSDCF) design and monitoring. 
As a result of increased awareness, CTUA has stepped up its engagement with the UN Resident Coordinator Office and in September 2020 the CTUA was elected in Armenia&apos;s National SDG council, namely in its working group on social and employment issues.</narrative>
   </description>
